@MilosKozak I am not sure why that code was put their in the first place, and would appreciate understanding that a bit better before doing that. 

@bartsopers do you remember why you chose to ignore certain time change events in this [function](https://github.com/nightscout/AndroidAPS/blob/65b0c5ccda53e2533c6b987254c0dcc6d27a7661/omnipod/src/main/java/info/nightscout/androidaps/plugins/pump/omnipod/OmnipodPumpPlugin.java#L963)?

```
public void timezoneOrDSTChanged(TimeChangeType timeChangeType) {
        aapsLogger.info(LTag.PUMP, "Time, Date and/or TimeZone changed. [changeType=" + timeChangeType.name() + ", eventHandlingEnabled=" + aapsOmnipodManager.isTimeChangeEventEnabled() + "]");

        if (timeChangeType == TimeChangeType.TimeChanged) {
            aapsLogger.info(LTag.PUMP, "Ignoring time change because it is not a DST or TZ change");
            return;
        } else if (!podStateManager.isPodRunning()) {
            aapsLogger.info(LTag.PUMP, "Ignoring time change because no Pod is active");
            return;
        }

        aapsLogger.info(LTag.PUMP, "DST and/or TimeZone changed event will be consumed by driver");
        hasTimeDateOrTimeZoneChanged = true;
    }
```

Edit: since it prevents non-DST or TZ time correction from happening, I would suggest removing.

@MilosKozak The removed code was implemented for the reasons mentioned in https://github.com/nightscout/AndroidAPS/pull/192

~I have a WIP PR  https://github.com/nightscout/AndroidAPS/pull/726 which currently just removes the offending code as in the patch, but I am thinking about how to lower the frequency without preventing them altogether.~

PR https://github.com/nightscout/AndroidAPS/pull/726 only updates time on the pod if the last event was more than 24 hours ago.
